### The parent challenges a father's punishment because it hurts or frightens the toddler.

2 of 27 posts.

One parent objects to a father repeatedly hitting a two-and-a-half-year-old on the chest and seeks internet evidence he might accept. Another describes a newly two-year-old screaming, shaking and slipping during a shower chosen because he hates it; the parent intervened and warned of leaving with the children and seeking a protective order. Both question a father's response to behaviour they do not think warrants it.

- "I have a 2.5 year old and any time he does something wrong his dad pops him on the chest." [ar081-0009]
- "My childs dad does not listen to what I say but will listen to videos and people on the internet so can I get some weigh in on this issue and what possible complications there are to hitting a 2.5 year old in the chest area." [ar081-0009]
- "My husband declared that my son was done and he was going to give him a shower instead of a bath because “he hates it.”" [ar033-0053]
- "I walked into the bathroom to room where said shower was being done and my son was screaming crying, shaking and scared. He slipped in the shower and could have seriously hurt himself." [ar033-0053]
- "I told him if he ever does that again, it would be the last time and I will be taking the children and filing a protective order." [ar033-0055]

**But:** The fathers dispute the parents' interpretation: one thinks the hitting will not hurt, and the other gives a practical explanation for the shower. "He denied it being abusive and said the he needed to be bathed and he was doing a shower because it’s faster." [ar033-0054]

