### The parent wants adults to respond to toddler behaviour without shaming, mocking or taking it personally.

6 of 27 posts.

Parents of children aged two to three-and-a-half describe grandparents or aunts treating crying, impulsivity, play and slow warming as deliberate wrongdoing or personal rejection. Responses include visible disgust, demands for an apology, withdrawal, mocking tears, insults and advice to threaten or frighten the child. Parents describe correcting behaviour themselves while wanting adults to understand the child's age; attempts range from explaining and intervening to ending a visit early.

- "My sister then accused me of letting her “get her way” and eventually called my daughter a “fucking cry baby” while she was standing right there." [ar001-0134]
- "his body language is like he's dealing with some really annoying dog or some dirt that he isn't able to tolerate.. my heart breaks for my son." [ar071-0005]
- "She also told him to apologize (which he didn't ) and then he tried to carry on with her like normal and she was like "we're not talking right now."" [ar038-0055]
- "While I was trying to calm her, my FIL got frustrated, stormed toward the boat and said, “Just throw her in.”" [ar006-0187]
- "Once, I was in the room when my mom said “aw don’t cry, you’re going to make grandma cry too!”" [ar009-0110]
- "My mom will tell me that I need to be harder on him and use a loud voice to assert dominance and tell him that his behaviour is not acceptable. To use scare tactics like he’s not going to eat dinner if he continues, or that I’m going to call a police officer on him." [ar049-0087]
- "We tried to set her up for success. We have talked with her for weeks about this trip. Read books. Told her it’s okay to feel shy and she doesn’t have to do anything she doesn’t want to. We acted scenarios out like saying hello or giving a high five. I tried to make everything as predictable as possible for her and prepare her for things." [ar006-0234]
- "Great news - I woke up yesterday and my husband had booked us a flight home and cut our trip short. He told us he was getting us out of there :’)" [ar006-0243]

**But:** Some distinguish harshness from firm correction they welcome: "My mum and mother-in-law can both be firm with her, and I’m okay with that because they are firm without being mean. They correct her behaviour but still make her feel safe and loved." [ar001-0135] One says the child currently appears unaffected: "She seems completely unfazed by it for now, but she is only 2." [ar009-0112]

