### The parent feels undermined when other caregivers give in or do not keep the same rules.

2 of 27 posts.

Two mothers of two-and-a-half- and three-and-a-half-year-olds describe becoming the adult who enforces limits while a partner, or a partner and grandmother, distracts or gives in. One links this to a partner's phone use, minimal engagement and leaving developmental transitions to her; the other describes the change after a new baby. They report resentment or fear that the older child now sees only mommy as stern.

- "Basically, he's the classic pushover parent with our 2.5 year old. I don't even feel that he's just spoiling her though because his only motive seems to be to do whatever it takes to stop her from crying so he can get back to his phone/napping/TV." [ar001-0031]
- "I tried implementing a no phone during the evening rule, but he just sleeps or watches TV instead." [ar001-0035]
- "Basically, I feel like I'm fighting against him at every turn to parent our daughter in a reasonable way. I don't know what to do because all I feel is resentment now." [ar001-0037]
- "Ever since bringing home our second son (2 months) my husband and MIL have gotten soft with our 3.5 year old, and it seems I'm the only person to discipline him anymore." [ar011-0055]
- "Add on the fact I am constantly breastfeeding the baby, so I have to tell him "not right now" a lot more than before. He hates me now, huh?" [ar011-0055]

**But:** One mother explicitly distinguishes the problem from immediate failures to keep the child alive or safe: "She's not in danger of dying around him. Like he's not leaving her unattended in the bathtub or not using a car seat or forgetting to feed her or anything. It's just general laziness/bare minimum." [ar001-0042]

