2 of 27 posts.

A parent of a two-and-a-half-year-old worries that anxious grandparents make physical exploration frightening, despite a helmet and supervision. A parent of a two-year-old runner has the opposite conflict: a friend invites the child to run while the parent teaches handholding and limits running near roads. Both want to set the conditions under which their child can practise moving more independently.

- "But my in laws freak out over everything I think and it might be giving him anxiety imo." [ar070-0024]
- "He wears a helmet, I’m always there, I do supervise activity I just don’t  stress to much about bumps and bruises, scrapes or cuts I don’t want him too either." [ar070-0026]
- "his grandma was like maybe get rid of the bell and I’m just like 🤨 maybe we just work on our balance more and he can still enjoy his bike bell." [ar070-0026]
- "I just want everyone’s option on the matter I’ve recently faced. My child is 2 and is a runner , I’m trying to teach him to walk next to me nicely without hand holding , he’s currently really good at holding hands and knows if he’s out the pram he has to hold my hand unless we are at the park etc." [ar026-0053]
- "Most recently we were walking towards the shops and her child ran ahead, she turned to my son who was holding my hand and said “ do you want to run too “ I just felt like this was so disrespectful to my boundaries as she’s well aware of what I’m trying to teach him and the fact he runs and doesn’t stop. What does everyone else think?" [ar026-0055]

