A parent of a two-and-a-half-year-old describes a long, precise bedtime routine and rocking after climbing forced a move out of the crib; the husband compares this with other toddlers asleep by seven. A parent of a six-month-old with reflux, CMPA and eczema rejects online pressure to sleep train, saying the pediatrician advises against it while the baby is in pain. Both seek understanding for the comfort they provide rather than treating independent sleep as the immediate requirement.

- "Putting my 2.5 year old to bed can legit take an hour or an hour and a half some nights. She has a very specific routine she wants which includes reading the same books in the same order, putting her stuffed animals in a specific order, then I rock her to sleep (yes, at age 2.5) and have to wait until she’s deep enough asleep that I can transfer her to her bed." [ar031-0027]
- "My husband met some random other toddler parents whose kids are asleep by 7pm sharp and now he’s convinced that we’re doing something wrong here." [ar031-0029]
- "And yes, it is frustrating. But I don’t think I can bring myself to “re sleep train” a 2.5 year old. Eventually she won’t need me to rock her anymore. Right?? I’m basically rambling but I can’t be the only one out there whose toddler takes forever to get to sleep?" [ar031-0030]
- "This is because of his reflux, cmpa, and eczema and his own pediatrician said he advises against sleep training my son since he has discomforts." [ar049-0046]
- "Yet I get comments and comments of people telling me “stop complaining and sleep train” “enjoy your misery”" [ar049-0046]
- "He needs me right now and I refuse to force him to sleep alone and make him cry and cry when I know he wants comfort from me." [ar049-0047]

**But:** The toddler's parent describes independent sleep before the crib transition, rather than a child who has always required rocking: "Honestly, she used to be a great independent sleeper and didn’t need to rock her or anything. Just put her in her crib and she went to sleep on her own. That all went out the window as soon as she started climbing out of her crib, so we had to transition her out of it." [ar031-0028]

