### The parent questions caregivers who label the toddler's distress or progress as a problem.

2 of 27 posts.

Parents of a recently two-year-old and a child aged two years ten months question how childcare staff speak about regulation, toilet learning and fear. One worries that direct pressure about pooping in a diaper may contribute to renewed stool withholding; the other says five days at a new daycare after a move is very little time to expect easy separation. They want support for the child while resisting descriptions such as scaredy cat or extreme.

- "Except that I remember that morning, the teacher said hello and almost immediately after said “we have to get you potty trained so you don’t poop in your diaper” which I thought was an odd way to start the day. Now I’m wondering if that comment is contributing to him retaining again ?" [ar022-0121]
- "This evening, my husband came home and said the teacher told him my son was a scaredy cat because of not liking to play hide and seek. I don’t like the communication style at all." [ar022-0123]
- "Am I overreacting? I just think it’s something to communicate to the parent, and not the child." [ar022-0122]
- "She has only had 5 full days at the new place so far and the daycare caregivers are like “she needs to learn to regulate” and seem alarmed by her behavior. My partner kind of feeds into this and is like “yes she can be very extreme at home as well” and I’m like …..she is 2?" [ar057-0098]
