### The parent asks whether public criticism expects more self-control than a toddler can manage.

2 of 27 posts.

Parents describe being criticised during a two-and-a-half-year-old's lobby meltdown and a three-year-old's loud singing late in a four-hour flight. Both had already tried to manage the behaviour and ask whether the stranger's expectations were reasonable. One describes humiliation and anxiety about seeing the neighbour again; the other points to turbulence and limited ways to redirect a confined child.

- "I got down to his level, said what was happening, offered a choice, and started taking slow breaths with him." [ar006-0133]
- ""That is why kids are the way they are now. He needs discipline. If that was my kid he would not dare."" [ar006-0133]
- "My face went hot and I just froze. I managed to say, "He's two," and picked him up. I was shaking the whole elevator ride and then I cried in my kitchen. I know toddlers have meltdowns and that I did nothing wrong, but being called out in that echoey lobby felt humiliating, like getting a public report card on my parenting." [ar006-0134]
- "Around the 3 hour mark, he started singing a bit loudly. This continued for about 10 minutes despite our attempts to ask him to be quiet and redirect him." [ar029-0057]
- "She then asks can we take him for a walk or something, to which I replied no because the seatbelt sign was on for turbulence…" [ar029-0057]
- "Like I’m sorry, you cannot reasonably tell me you have never experienced a time when your toddler didn’t listen to you. Not to mention, when you have limited options to redirect them when they’ve been cooped up on an airplane for over 3 hours. Her tone was also super rude and judgmental - like her kids are never misbehaved and are perfect angels. Someone please tell me if I’m in the wrong here." [ar029-0058]

**But:** The airline account acknowledges an actual disturbance rather than only anticipated judgement: "Then, the woman sitting in front of him turns around and says can we please do something about him because he woke her up." [ar029-0057]

