### The parent wants to protect the child's interests without teaching shame about girls' and boys' toys.

1 of 27 posts.

A parent bought a Hello Kitty plushie for a son turning four, who now takes it everywhere. The father's worry that other children might mock a boy with a girls' toy makes the parent question the decision, while wanting the child to be himself. No actual bullying is reported.

- "I want to teach my son it’s okay to be himself too and to not be ashamed of the things he likes! This is why I bought the toy for him in the first place!" [ar037-0013]
- "He is worried our son will get bullied for being attached to a “girls toy”." [ar037-0005]
- "Like someone down below said, it’s usually adults who project that on children, so I personally don’t see any harm in him having a “girls” toy. But I also don’t want him to be laughed at by other children since I have no clue what their parents are teaching them at home." [ar037-0008]
- "So naturally, for Easter, I got one for him. He is overjoyed. He takes her everywhere, shows her everything, feeds her and plays with her all the time. I find his happiness adorable." [ar037-0003]

**But:** The father also enjoys his son's happiness and is not objecting to the toy itself: "Yeah, my husband was also happy to see our son happy. He doesn’t have a problem with him carrying her around and involving her in every small daily task." [ar037-0015]

