### The parent wants a grandmother to stop teaching things that conflict with the family's values.

3 of 27 posts.

Parents of two- to three-and-a-half-year-olds describe a grandmother passing on Catholic teachings against their wishes, encouraging taking belongings, or accidentally teaching a culturally rude hand gesture. They want to decide what their child learns and how to explain the boundary without unnecessary conflict. The gesture was caught before the toddler learned it, while the other two parents anticipate continued disagreement or future babysitting.

- "I approached my mom about this the other day and of course I was upset. She also got upset and said that it is her duty to pass the faith onto her grandchild and that she made that promise to the priest when my daughter got baptized. She also asked to take my daughter to her church group and I said no." [ar074-0025]
- "I’m just wondering if I’m explaining this in the best way possible to my daughter in a way that she can understand. I said that those are granny’s special beliefs that bring her comfort. I explained that those are granny’s beliefs and that’s ok, however other people have different beliefs, and that’s also ok." [ar074-0026]
- "Basically, a few weeks ago I invited my MIL to join us on a trip to a local children’s museum. While we were my MIL basically stole one of the toys from the play area. She pulled it out of her pocket while I was buckling my child into her seat and gave it to her." [ar016-0039]
- "I’m super irritated that she would think it’s ok to teach my toddler this. How do I confront the situation next time? Should I tell my husband to reach out and set this boundary? She’s supposed to be babysitting for us when I start my new job and soon this is adding onto the worry, especially because she’s not someone who typically responds well to criticism." [ar016-0041]
- "Just caught my (Chinese) MIL trying to teach my 2yo the victory sign..." [ar041-0146]
- "...in a way us Brits would recognise as a 'two fingered salute'." [ar041-0147]
- "She was mortified when I said that was a rather rude gesture. Thankfully I caught them before the 2yo figured it out." [ar041-0148]

**But:** The religious objection allows learning about different beliefs, and the gesture incident appears accidental: "I’m fine with my daughter learning about religion at this age, but I’d rather it be in the context that there are many different belief systems." [ar074-0027] "She was mortified when I said that was a rather rude gesture." [ar041-0148]

